@flexiarg apparatus/done-is-observed-running
@title Done Is Observed Running
@keywords activation, deployment, manifest, comparator, stale-jvm, live-state
@audience apparatus builders, reviewers, futon developers
@tone technical
@style pattern
@status proposed (extracted from the APM failure record 2026-09-08; claude-9 draft, claude-1 redline; pending Joe's ruling)
@references [apparatus/one-authority-per-question apparatus/monitors-measure-the-work]
@see-also apparatus/the-system-stops-on-schedule
@why APM, four instances in one night (2026-09-07/08): reopen-posthoc-rejection — the intended apparatus-repair recovery — written and tested with no production caller anywhere; a substrate fix committed but never loaded; a display fix committed but never loaded; topology-supervisor.sh, written specifically so loop stoppages would not reach Joe, not running — which is why the stoppage reached Joe. Register A11/M7: commits reaching disk but not the serving JVM present as inexplicable intermittent failures. Generalizes Joe's I_data_current rule from instruments to mechanisms.
@how own mechanism: every mechanism declares itself in a repo-owned manifest; one standing comparator, on a timer, diffs declared-should-run against live-is-running and loaded-vs-committed, and a mismatch is a first-class apparatus fault.
@violation-signature A declaration with zero production callers; a fix that works in the repo and not in the process; a mechanism written to prevent X while X reaches the operator; compliance demonstrated by a one-off scan rather than a standing check.
! conclusion: A mechanism exists when the live system shows it acting, not when the commit lands; the gap between declared, loaded, and running is closed by a standing comparator, not by discipline.

  + context: Fixes and safety mechanisms are produced faster than they are activated: namespaces need reloading, daemons need starting, recovery routes need callers.

  + IF:
    Correctness depends on something being switched on — a loaded namespace, a running supervisor, a wired production caller.

  + HOWEVER:
    The definition of done in use is "committed and reviewed", so the repo and the live system silently diverge; and the occasional audit that catches a gap is a one-off scan, which FEELS like compliance but is true exactly once.

  + THEN:
    Make activation part of done: a packet delivering a mechanism is complete when the reviewer has observed the mechanism act in the live system. Structurally, maintain a manifest of what should be running and one comparator on a timer diffing it against what is running and what is loaded; mismatches are apparatus faults like any other.

  + BECAUSE:
    Two sources of truth with no comparator (see apparatus/one-authority-per-question) is exactly what repo-vs-live is; discipline is what you substitute for the missing comparator, and discipline does not survive contact with a live campaign.

    + evidence: APM: the four same-night instances above; the 2026-08-28 stale-JVM mint failure closed as "succeeded on retry" when a manual reload was the actual fix. WM: F10's rider gap ("declaration exists, zero production callers") found 2026-09-08 by a one-off scan — principle 8 done once, not as a mechanism.

  + NEXT-STEPS:
    - Build the manifest + timed comparator for your apparatus's daemons, recovery routes, and loaded namespaces.
    - Add to review checklists: "where did you watch this act, live?"
